  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 345 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The chance for severe weather has diminished across \nVirginia, but remains in place across NE NC through early evening. \nLocalized flooding remains possible along and south of I-64 through \ntonight, though confidence has decreased some since this morning.\n\nAfternoon wx analysis shows that the cold front has pushed through \nmost of the area. Instability has been limited due to 2 factors: the \nfront to our south and the fact that convection moved across central \nand SE Virginia earlier than expected. Strong to severe storms have \ndeveloped across western and north-central NC, and could clip far SE \nportions of the area between now and 7 PM. A Severe Thunderstorm \nWatch has been issued from Bertie to Currituck Counties (along the \nnorth side of the Albemarle Sound) to account for this threat. While \nthere will likely be additional development north of the front from \nthe RIC Metro to Hampton Roads, severe wx is not likely though can't \ncompletely rule out localized flooding if showers/storms train over \nurban areas. The threat for flooding will gradually decrease this \nevening and tonight as the intensity of the convection wanes (though \nscattered showers likely continue through the night, especially \nacross SW portions of the FA). \n\nShower/storm chances decrease Sun (15-40% PoPs) with most convection \nlikely confined to the far southern portions of the area (or \nremaining south altogether). Beyond Sun, dry conditions are expected \nearly next week, likely continuing into midweek. The next chance of \nrain appears to arrive on Thursday or Friday ahead of a cold front, \nthough guidance is not overly optimistic regarding this.",
